  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nUpper low continues to spin over the Four Corners region while the \nmain westerlies are along the US-Canada border. The sfc low has been \nslowly moving through the OK/TX panhandles into southwest KS early \nthis afternoon. Strong synoptic lift driven by WAA and divergence \naloft has brought a broad area of continuous light rain with pockets \nof moderate to heavy rain at times this morning and afternoon, all \nwithin an environment comprised of Pwat around 2\" with the moisture \naxis overhead. Today's rain has mostly impacted central and parts of \nnortheast KS, much of the same areas that saw the heaviest rainfall \nlast night. Washington, Marshall, and Nemaha Counties had a swath of \n2-3\" last night into this morning with light rain lingering into \nthis afternoon, so this area would be the most primed for flooding \nissues with any additional heavy rainfall between now and Thursday. \n\nThe warm front has washed out with the ongoing rain and is not well-\ndefined. Our sfc winds are mainly southerly, but are backed to the \nsoutheast towards central KS (closer to the center of the low). \nVisible satellite shows the dry slot working its way through \nsouthwest KS, and the thinking is once that arrives into central KS, \nthat should allow for better destabilization with SBCAPE between 500-\n1000 J/kg. It remains questionable how much time the atmosphere will \nhave to become unstable enough to develop severe storms in our area, \nbut the increasing LLJ late this afternoon into early evening will \nonly help low-level shear profiles. With veering winds, 0-3km shear \naround 40 kts and 0-3km SRH around 200 m2/s2, it may not take as \nmuch instability as one would expect to get a strong enough storm to \nproduce a quick tornado or two. The window of opportunity looks \nrather short, especially as our area would be on the eastern fringes \nof the threat and would take longer to see enough clearing, but will \nneed to look out for this potential particularly between 4-8pm today.\n\nOutside of the severe risk, heavy rain and flooding remains the \nbiggest threat especially for those areas that already received \nheavy rain as mentioned earlier. It wouldn't take much additional \nrainfall for the northernmost counties in the watch to exacerbate \nflooding issues or lead to more flooding. Once the bulk of the rain \nthis evening moves north and east of the area, there should be a \nmore notable break overnight, although a few scattered showers are \nstill possible along the KS/NE border. Closer to daybreak Thursday, \nanother surge pushes north out of OK into east central KS and \ncoverage also increases along the cold front stretched across \nnorthern KS. This front will gradually move west to east across the \narea throughout the day, leading to wet weather area-wide. The \nheaviest rain should come to an end in northern KS during the \nafternoon, while east central KS should see continued rain into the \nearly evening, becoming lighter later into the night. A general 1.5 \nto 3\" with locally higher amounts around 4\" should be expected on \ntop of what has already fallen. Some of this is in locations outside \nof the watch, with the 12z HREF showing some high-end potential for \n3-4\" south of I-35. However, this area is not nearly as saturated as \nthose further north, so opted to leave the Flood Watch as is.\n\nThe upper system weakens into Friday with the front passing east, \nleading to a transition to zonal and eventually northwest flow \naloft. Several days of dry weather are expected, a welcome change to \nhelp us dry out after the wet weather this week. Temperatures will \nbe coolest on Friday immediately behind the boundary with highs in \nthe 60s followed by lows in the 40s Saturday morning. We then warm \nslightly with highs in the 70s and lows in the 50s the rest of the \nforecast period.",
